1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:06,480
So if you manage digital systems for a government ministry, you, uh, you definitely know the consequences of poor design.

2
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:08,340
Oh, absolutely. It's a headache.

3
00:00:08,820 --> 00:00:11,760
Yeah. Because without a clear overview, systems just quickly become,

4
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:12,720
well,

5
00:00:13,340 --> 00:00:18,180
fragmented or siloed or duplicated, or you end up locked in to one supplier.

6
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:21,960
Right. And when that happens, they become incredibly costly to change.

7
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:22,640
Exactly.

8
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:26,540
So for today's deep dive, we're looking at a solution to that exact problem.

9
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:32,299
We're unpacking how massive institutions can objectively evaluate their infrastructure.

10
00:00:32,740 --> 00:00:34,980
And we're doing that using two specific tools.

11
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:41,500
First, the UNDP Digital Development Compass, which is, uh, essentially a standardized grading scale.

12
00:00:41,780 --> 00:00:49,480
Right. And then GovStack's reference architecture, which acts as the blueprint for what good digital government actually looks like.

13
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:54,600
Perfect. But before we get into the mechanics of that, I have a core problem with this.

14
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:55,340
Okay. What's that?

15
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,020
Why do we need rigid,

16
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:01,620
published, written criteria to grade these systems?

17
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:08,860
I mean, doesn't forcing a messy real-world government into a strict mathematical rubric strip away all the nuance?

18
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:10,080
You would think so, yeah.

19
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:14,620
Like, why can't we just trust our senior engineers to assess the systems based on their own experience?

20
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:19,000
Well, because experience usually translates to subjective interpretation.

21
00:01:19,460 --> 00:01:25,620
I mean, if two assessors look at the exact same database and reach different conclusions, their score isn't a fact.

22
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:27,760
Right. It's just an opinion.

23
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:35,880
Exactly. It's merely an opinion. And in government, an opinion can simply be set aside by a minister who, you know, maybe doesn't like the result.

24
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:37,760
Ah, so they just ignore it.

25
00:01:38,060 --> 00:01:38,460
Pretty much.

26
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:43,880
So written criteria on a published scale, that forces an objective truth.

27
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:49,620
It mathematically removes optimism and ensures everyone is looking at the exact same reality.

28
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:54,280
Okay, so by removing that human optimism, how does the actual scale function?

29
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:56,620
Like, how do we calculate that objective truth?

30
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:01,440
So it relies on five published stages. You have basic, which is zero to one.

31
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:04,160
Okay. Then opportunistic, that's one to two.

32
00:02:04,980 --> 00:02:08,220
Systematic is two to three. Differentiating is three to four.

33
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:11,220
And finally, transformational, which is four to five.

34
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:18,840
But wait, if an assessor is evaluating a massive domain, how do they definitively score a one point five versus, say, a one point seven?

35
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:20,720
Yeah, that's the tricky part.

36
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:25,420
It-- Because it feels like we're just inviting subjective decimals right back into the process.

37
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:31,680
Well, they prevent that by using a strict four-part scoring rule that completely eliminates subjective decimals.

38
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:33,660
Okay, so what's rule number one?

39
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:37,920
First, subcomponents have written criteria at three specific levels.

40
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:41,540
So that's basic, systematic, and transformational.

41
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:47,660
If you only partially meet the criteria for a higher stage, the math automatically drops you to the lower tier.

42
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:49,560
Got it. And the second part?

43
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:53,660
Second, assessors are only allowed to score verified positions.

44
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:55,460
Meaning you need hard proof.

45
00:02:55,700 --> 00:02:59,560
Precisely. You can't just take a vendor's word that a system is, like, transformational.

46
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:00,040
Got it.

47
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:04,660
Third, every single stage is pegged exactly to the middle of its unit.

48
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:09,140
So basic is always zero point five. Opportunistic is one point five.

49
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,400
Systematic is two point five, and so on.

50
00:03:11,780 --> 00:03:13,660
You will never see a one point seven.

51
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:16,120
Oh, wow. So it's totally locked in.

52
00:03:16,460 --> 00:03:16,840
Exactly.

53
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:23,220
And fourth, a domain's final score is just the mean average of its subcomponents calculated to one decimal place.

54
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:27,980
Okay, so when a government is forced to look in the mirror using this strict math,

55
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:31,780
what actually happens? Let's look at the Progresa simulation.

56
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:33,040
That's a great example.

57
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:37,520
And we should note explicitly, Progresa is an entirely fictional country.

58
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:39,520
It's just used to model this framework.

59
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:42,060
How did this objective scoring play out for them?

60
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:47,320
Beautifully, actually. Because the whole picture of their digital infrastructure fits on one table.

61
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:48,680
Wow, just one table.

62
00:03:49,140 --> 00:03:56,320
Yeah. For example, their digital data domain sits at a one point three, which places it squarely in the opportunistic stage.

63
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:00,800
And that one point three is just the direct mathematical average we just discussed.

64
00:04:01,160 --> 00:04:09,460
Yes. They had five subcomponents that scored one point five, and one basic component regarding data quality that scored zero point five.

65
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:11,860
Okay, and the mean gives you exactly one point three.

66
00:04:12,300 --> 00:04:19,140
Exactly. And because this is mapped against the reference architecture, the government can identify a clear, actionable priority.

67
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:21,079
Which was what in their case?

68
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:28,260
Making the PLR, you know, their central learner registry, making that the authoritative register for educational data.

69
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:32,680
What really stands out to me is how this strict verification catches blind spots.

70
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:40,140
Like Progresa's original unverified desk draft had their interoperability score at a two point three, but the real score was lower.

71
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:48,740
It was, yeah. When assessors actually went looking for verified evidence, they discovered the Ministry of Education wasn't actually plugged into LinkUp.

72
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:51,280
LinkUp being Progresa's national data exchange.

73
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:56,340
Right. And because of that verified fact, the score dropped to two point one.

74
00:04:57,040 --> 00:05:01,100
The data reflected the actual reality, not just the intended design.

75
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:03,740
Which perfectly illustrates the elegance of this framework.

76
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:10,200
By bringing a massive, complex domain down to a single verified table, you completely eliminate the guesswork.

77
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:12,200
You really do. It's all right there.

